<section class="section section--dark section--roomy">
  <div class="wrap" style="text-align:left">
    <h1>Welcome to the Stroud Lab.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Why are there so many species, and why do they live where they live?
    
      We are an evolutionary ecology lab with broad interests, unified by a single
      theme: that ecology and evolution are not separable. Behavior and ecology are
      the processes; the staggering diversity of life across space and through time
      is the pattern. The central ambition of the lab is to collect data in the
      field on the observable processes that explain large-scale patterns of
      diversity.
    
      Two questions capture the flavor of this. How does individual- and
      population-level ecology and behavior structure entire communities? And how
      does natural selection explain why some species end up looking, behaving, or
      performing the way they do, and so which species coexist? We are interested in
      the mechanistic link between the small and the large, the proximate and the
      ultimate.
    
      Nearly all of our work is built on lizards, and mostly on <em>Anolis</em>.
      That is a deliberate choice. Anoles are diverse, they live at high densities
      in multi-species communities, their natural history is exceptionally well
      understood, and, most usefully of all, they are easy to watch. There are now
      full genome sequences and CRISPR-Cas9 approaches for anoles. And we can drive
      to a very large number of study sites across the southeastern United States,
      which makes the logistics of long-term field work tractable in a way they
      rarely are.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <figure>
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/sagrei-pair.jpg" alt="Two brown anoles on a mossy tree trunk">
        <figcaption>Brown anoles, <em>Anolis sagrei</em>, on Lizard Island.</figcaption>
