      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The long-term study</p>
        <h2>Lizard Island</h2>
        <p>
          Since Fall 2015 we have sampled the entire anole community of a small
          island in Fairchild Tropical Botanic Garden (Miami, Florida). Originally,
          the community consisted of one semi-terrestrial specialist (the Cuban
          brown anole, <em>Anolis sagrei</em>), one tree trunk specialist 
          (Hispaniolan bark anole, <em>Anolis distichus</em>), and two arboreal 
          canopy specialists (the native American green anole, <em>Anolis carolinensis</em>),
          and the "crown-giant" Cuban knight anole, <em>Anolis equestris</em>). In
          late 2017, a 5th species joined the community: the Puerto Rican crested
          anole (<em>Anolis cristatellus</em>), another semi-terrestrial specialist--
          and we have been watching its evolutionary battle with the brown anole
          population ever since. On Lizard Island, there are approximately 800 to 
          1,000 lizards at any given time. We return every spring (May/June) and 
          every fall (Oct/Nov), which lets us measure selection separately
          through the summer reproductive season and the winter non-reproductive
          season.
        </p>
        <p>
          Long-term field studies measuring natural selection in multiple species 
          are realtively rare--the most famous example is Peter and Rosemary Grant's
          exceptional field study of Darwin's finches on Daphne Major in the Galapagos.
          On Lizard Island, we are trying to emulate that study: already, it is now
          one of the longest-running multi-species field studies of natural selection
          in the world currently active.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/research/">More on the Lizard Island study</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

